Data Structures Explained

  The main structure is stty, which encompasses a series of stty_value structs, that are both 
  grouped together and split on flag type (i.e. cflags, ccflags, iflags, lflags, etc.) The grouping
  is in a member called all_flags. Additionally, there are positional lengths, where: index 0 is lflags, 
  index 1 is iflags, index 2 is control characters, and index 3 is cflags. From this we can access each
  position and identify the length of flags, providing help for iterating through the list of flags.

  As mentioned above, the stty struct contains stty_values. These are the values that are normally found 
  if we call `pstty -a`, such as (igncr, echo, icanon, etc.) Each of these flags are accessible through 
  their name (how the flag is displayed) and through their key (the constant representing the integer 
  value of the flag). That being said, there is also a member that represents what is essentially a 
  lambda function, through a function pointer called (*value_handler)(). This function pointer points
  to a function that is associated with the value that handles flipping flags, setting control characters, 
  and setting integer values of the stty value, depending on the attribute type.

